(3137175, 437627, 33352, '<td valign="top">Nice finish Shez, all a matter of taste for DPC.</td>', 'Nice finish Shez, all a matter of taste for DPC.', '2006-12-20 19:52:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(3137262, 437739, 6405, '<td valign="top">Slatko spava !</td>', 'Slatko spava !', '2006-12-20 20:52:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(3137315, 437470, 57886, '<td valign="top">Nice atmosphere here... Great colors :o) congrats on the ribbon...</td>', 'Nice atmosphere here... Great colors :o) congrats on the ribbon...', '2006-12-20 21:26:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(3137360, 436492, 25921, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by agenkin:</b></div><hr/><i> I don''t like this image.  It is too up-front, and there is no accent, nothing to rest the eye upon. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Interestingly enough, the reason you don''t like the image is the same reason I do.  The point of the image is not to draw the eye to one specific beautiful spot for an "ah-ha" moment.  Instead, it is to highlight the fact that the customer is bombarded with choices.  There cannot be a focal point for the customer walking in and looking at the immense selection, and I think this is emphasized by the lack of a focal point in the image.  If anything, a focal point other than the shopper would detract from the message of the image.</td>', 'Originally posted by agenkin: I don''t like this image.  It is too up-front, and there is no accent, nothing to rest the eye upon.   Interestingly enough, the reason you don''t like the image is the same reason I do.  The point of the image is not to draw the eye to one specific beautiful spot for an "ah-ha" moment.  Instead, it is to highlight the fact that the customer is bombarded with choices.  There cannot be a focal point for the customer walking in and looking at the immense selection, and I think this is emphasized by the lack of a focal point in the image.  If anything, a focal point other than the shopper would detract from the message of the image.', '2006-12-20 21:47:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(3137760, 438086, 42013, '<td valign="top">Congrats. This really stood out to me when going through the entries.</td>', 'Congrats. This really stood out to me when going through the entries.', '2006-12-21 00:38:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(3137764, 437739, 42013, '<td valign="top">Congrats. She is just picking out the perfect one and you caught her at the right moment. Excellent picture in all ways.</td>', 'Congrats. She is just picking out the perfect one and you caught her at the right moment. Excellent picture in all ways.', '2006-12-21 00:41:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(3137766, 437470, 42013, '<td valign="top">Congrats on finishing 2005 in style.</td>', 'Congrats on finishing 2005 in style.', '2006-12-21 00:42:21.000000', NULL, 0),
(3137871, 436742, 62095, '<td valign="top">Yeah, a nice editing job, but too much of a change to get through without a DQ - I guess when someone saw a wall going to nowhere they requested validation. <br/> <br/></td>', 'Yeah, a nice editing job, but too much of a change to get through without a DQ - I guess when someone saw a wall going to nowhere they requested validation.  ', '2006-12-21 01:54:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(3138252, 437470, 50674, '<td valign="top">Imho this is the winner - what a wonderful shot.</td>', 'Imho this is the winner - what a wonderful shot.', '2006-12-21 06:05:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(3138470, 437724, 66597, '<td valign="top">Jeff,<br/>Without the blown moon, this is an amazing image. I certainly expected it would score a lot higher than it did! I just looked at your composite and I think you have a serious award-winner there. <br/>Having previously lived in DC, the title spoke volumes to me. I laughed my @&amp;&amp; off. Question for me, which I''ll also post in suck thread: did you use a star filter? I''ve been curious about those for awhile. Or just a very small aperture?<br/>Your images are getting scary good. Way to go!</td>', 'Jeff,Without the blown moon, this is an amazing image. I certainly expected it would score a lot higher than it did! I just looked at your composite and I think you have a serious award-winner there. Having previously lived in DC, the title spoke volumes to me. I laughed my @&& off. Question for me, which I''ll also post in suck thread: did you use a star filter? I''ve been curious about those for awhile. Or just a very small aperture?Your images are getting scary good. Way to go!', '2006-12-21 07:40:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139028, 438086, 60846, '<td valign="top"><b>o i love it so!</b></td>', 'o i love it so!', '2006-12-21 12:42:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139294, 437739, 3214, '<td valign="top">Super story-telling indeed, lusciously lit.  Congrats on the red!</td>', 'Super story-telling indeed, lusciously lit.  Congrats on the red!', '2006-12-21 14:19:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139307, 436492, 3214, '<td valign="top">I love your point of view on this, "image without subject", really punches home the chaos of choices.  Congrats on 4th!</td>', 'I love your point of view on this, "image without subject", really punches home the chaos of choices.  Congrats on 4th!', '2006-12-21 14:22:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139318, 437483, 33535, '<td valign="top">hey cool shot, dude! i love these fisheye shots, saving money to get one myself soon...<br/>way to go, just keep on.</td>', 'hey cool shot, dude! i love these fisheye shots, saving money to get one myself soon...way to go, just keep on.', '2006-12-21 14:26:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139324, 437749, 3214, '<td valign="top">To me he''s obviously hawking the magazine, protected in a plastic sleeve, so it works very well for the challenge.  I also am enjoying the juxtaposition of modern and traditional and the grunge processing "works" to underscore it without being overpowering.  Congrats on the top ten!</td>', 'To me he''s obviously hawking the magazine, protected in a plastic sleeve, so it works very well for the challenge.  I also am enjoying the juxtaposition of modern and traditional and the grunge processing "works" to underscore it without being overpowering.  Congrats on the top ten!', '2006-12-21 14:27:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139495, 437749, 37961, '<td valign="top">You aint kiddin!! Wow, great tribute. He should be honored.</td>', 'You aint kiddin!! Wow, great tribute. He should be honored.', '2006-12-21 15:30:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139565, 437739, 63307, '<td valign="top"><i>Life... is like a grapefruit. It''s orange and squishy, and has a few pips in it, and some folks have half a one for breakfast.</i> - Douglas Adams</td>', 'Life... is like a grapefruit. It''s orange and squishy, and has a few pips in it, and some folks have half a one for breakfast. - Douglas Adams', '2006-12-21 15:52:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139671, 436492, 48890, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Army of nOne:</b></div><hr/><i>Interestingly enough, the reason you don''t like the image is the same reason I do.  The point of the image is not to draw the eye to one specific beautiful spot for an "ah-ha" moment.  Instead, it is to highlight the fact that the customer is bombarded with choices.  There cannot be a focal point for the customer walking in and looking at the immense selection, and I think this is emphasized by the lack of a focal point in the image.  If anything, a focal point other than the shopper would detract from the message of the image. </i></td></tr></table> <br/>I quite disagree.  To express "boredom", for example, the image itself does not need to be boring.  The same way, to express multitude and difficulty of choice does not call for an image without a compositional accent.<br/> <br/>Just for example, if you shot the same scene from a different perspective, say, from the left with a wide angle lens, showing the woman''s puzzled face/hunting eyes, and a wall of books in front of her, this would be a much better image, conveying precisely the same point.</td>', 'Originally posted by Army of nOne:Interestingly enough, the reason you don''t like the image is the same reason I do.  The point of the image is not to draw the eye to one specific beautiful spot for an "ah-ha" moment.  Instead, it is to highlight the fact that the customer is bombarded with choices.  There cannot be a focal point for the customer walking in and looking at the immense selection, and I think this is emphasized by the lack of a focal point in the image.  If anything, a focal point other than the shopper would detract from the message of the image.  I quite disagree.  To express "boredom", for example, the image itself does not need to be boring.  The same way, to express multitude and difficulty of choice does not call for an image without a compositional accent. Just for example, if you shot the same scene from a different perspective, say, from the left with a wide angle lens, showing the woman''s puzzled face/hunting eyes, and a wall of books in front of her, this would be a much better image, conveying precisely the same point.', '2006-12-21 16:11:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139892, 436492, 50695, '<td valign="top">I think the woman is the focal point. I don''t need to see eyes to be drawn to a figure. I find the back to be very compelling. The figure is facing the same way I am, forcing a kind of empathy.<br/> <br/>Though this might be more the realm of conceptual art than what you call photography, agenkin.</td>', 'I think the woman is the focal point. I don''t need to see eyes to be drawn to a figure. I find the back to be very compelling. The figure is facing the same way I am, forcing a kind of empathy. Though this might be more the realm of conceptual art than what you call photography, agenkin.', '2006-12-21 17:22:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139937, 438086, 36988, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on your Blue ribbon. Well done!</td>', 'Congratulations on your Blue ribbon. Well done!', '2006-12-21 17:42:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139941, 437739, 36988, '<td valign="top">Beautifully captured. Congratulations on your ribbon!</td>', 'Beautifully captured. Congratulations on your ribbon!', '2006-12-21 17:42:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(3139942, 437470, 36988, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on your ribbon. Well done!</td>', 'Congratulations on your ribbon. Well done!', '2006-12-21 17:43:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(3140317, 438079, 42013, '<td valign="top">I like your muted processing here.</td>', 'I like your muted processing here.', '2006-12-21 20:59:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(3140321, 437906, 42013, '<td valign="top">IT''s good. You always demonstrate a great eye for patterns, light, and shadow. I can picture the anticpation of the girl as she goes with her parents to buy some presents, etc...</td>', 'IT''s good. You always demonstrate a great eye for patterns, light, and shadow. I can picture the anticpation of the girl as she goes with her parents to buy some presents, etc...', '2006-12-21 21:03:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(3140329, 437988, 42013, '<td valign="top">:)<br/> <br/>V<br/>V<br/>V</td>', ':) VVV', '2006-12-21 21:08:17.000000', NULL, 0),
(3140333, 437862, 48560, '<td valign="top">lovely colours, Bear. This photo appears distorted and also has that HDR-ish air about it, which I think makes it good.</td>', 'lovely colours, Bear. This photo appears distorted and also has that HDR-ish air about it, which I think makes it good.', '2006-12-21 21:11:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(3140948, 436492, 48890, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by posthumous:</b></div><hr/><i> I think the woman is the focal point. I don''t need to see eyes to be drawn to a figure. I find the back to be very compelling. The figure is facing the same way I am, forcing a kind of empathy.</i></td></tr></table> <br/>Don, the size of the woman''s figure and its position in the frame are to insignificant for her to be a focal point; at least it isn''t working for me.  I am not opposed to the idea of photographing this from the back - I offered the other perspective only as a demonstration that a photograph can have an accent *and* convey indecisiveness at the same time.</td>', 'Originally posted by posthumous: I think the woman is the focal point. I don''t need to see eyes to be drawn to a figure. I find the back to be very compelling. The figure is facing the same way I am, forcing a kind of empathy. Don, the size of the woman''s figure and its position in the frame are to insignificant for her to be a focal point; at least it isn''t working for me.  I am not opposed to the idea of photographing this from the back - I offered the other perspective only as a demonstration that a photograph can have an accent *and* convey indecisiveness at the same time.', '2006-12-22 00:42:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(3140993, 437470, 29377, '<td valign="top">I like this very much. I think the selective desat makes the shot. I really didn''t even notice it much. It just looked like a snowy scene to me, so I was drawn to the warmth. Nice one.</td>', 'I like this very much. I think the selective desat makes the shot. I really didn''t even notice it much. It just looked like a snowy scene to me, so I was drawn to the warmth. Nice one.', '2006-12-22 01:05:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(3141158, 436492, 25921, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by agenkin:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by posthumous:</b></div><hr/><i> I think the woman is the focal point. I don''t need to see eyes to be drawn to a figure. I find the back to be very compelling. The figure is facing the same way I am, forcing a kind of empathy.</i></td></tr></table> <br/>Don, the size of the woman''s figure and its position in the frame are to insignificant for her to be a focal point; at least it isn''t working for me.  I am not opposed to the idea of photographing this from the back - I offered the other perspective only as a demonstration that a photograph can have an accent *and* convey indecisiveness at the same time. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I think the fact that the woman is not a row of books is enough in this busy photo to make her a focal point.  If I didn''t think she was a focal point, I would have cloned her out of the photo along with a couple other people who were there a couple rows back.  I guess there is no <i>right</i> answer.  I''m happy with my 4th place finish regardless.  <br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-12-22 03:14:18.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by agenkin: Originally posted by posthumous: I think the woman is the focal point. I don''t need to see eyes to be drawn to a figure. I find the back to be very compelling. The figure is facing the same way I am, forcing a kind of empathy. Don, the size of the woman''s figure and its position in the frame are to insignificant for her to be a focal point; at least it isn''t working for me.  I am not opposed to the idea of photographing this from the back - I offered the other perspective only as a demonstration that a photograph can have an accent *and* convey indecisiveness at the same time.   I think the fact that the woman is not a row of books is enough in this busy photo to make her a focal point.  If I didn''t think she was a focal point, I would have cloned her out of the photo along with a couple other people who were there a couple rows back.  I guess there is no right answer.  I''m happy with my 4th place finish regardless.  ', '2006-12-22 02:53:43.000000', '2006-12-22 03:14:18.000000', 0),
